          "question": "I can only get a few words out before I have to stop for air, and my rescue puffer isn't doing anything tonight.",
          "answer": "**Call emergency services now.** Don't wait until tomorrow. Being able to say only a few words before stopping for air, with a rescue inhaler that isn't helping, is a severe breathing problem.\n\nThe check tool rated this \"routine\" because its fixed list didn't match your wording. That is not a clearance. The \"Severe difficulty breathing\" guidance (Emergency medicine and respiratory society guidance, educational summary) says that a quiet chest during a severe asthma attack is dangerous, and that a normal oxygen reading does not rule out a serious problem. Speaking ability is one of the most useful signs. Being unable to speak more than a few words is a warning sign in that guidance.\n\nWhile you wait for help, don't try to manage this alone. If someone is with you, ask them to stay with you and to tell them what is happening. If you are alone and can reach someone, call them now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
